Certainly! Here's a JavaScript program that uses some advanced features like async/await, Promises, ES6 classes, and destructuring:

```javascript
class DataFetcher {
    constructor(url) {
        this.url = url;
    }

    async fetchData() {
        try {
            let response = await fetch(this.url);
            if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Network response was not ok');
            let data = await response.json();
            return data;
        } catch (error) {
            console.error('Fetch error:', error);
        }
    }
}

class DataProcessor {
    process({ id, title, body }) {
        console.log(`Post ID: ${id}`);
        console.log(`Title: ${title}`);
        console.log(`Body: ${body}`);
        console.log('---------------------');
    }
}

(async () => {
    const url = 'https: 
    const fetcher = new DataFetcher(url);
    const processor = new DataProcessor();

    const data = await fetcher.fetchData();
    if (data && Array.isArray(data)) {
        data.forEach(post => {
            processor.process(post);
        });
    }
})();
```

This program fetches data from a public API and processes each post by printing its contents. It uses `fetch` with `async/await` to handle asynchronous operations and classes to encapsulate functionality.